WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Back to the Future Part 2 and 3!

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.560
Two action-packed adventures in one great game!

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:30.960
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.880
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.060
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:41.360
The game wants you to believe, based on that back of the box, that this is a deal you couldn't possibly pass up.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.960
But is this a good deal?

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.240
Just before you even talk about the game itself, is this a good deal?

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:54.700
Or do you assume, because they have now condensed one video game into two movies, do you assume it's going to be half-assed instead?

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.740
Or are you like, oh, this is going to be two totally different games?

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.200
Let's be honest. Let's be honest.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:14.160
That the amount of, you know, cultural zeitgeist that two has, it's multiples of what three, I think, you know, at least in modern memory.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:22.660
So I think, like, if we're talking about how much of each movie is in this game, I think, like, it's the appropriate ratio.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.360
I think I recall Joe being a three apologist.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.200
I am a three apologist.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.880
I was just about to apologize.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.740
But I, um, I do think two is better than three.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.460
But I think three is good.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.500
And I think that...

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:42.140
Yeah, three's good. It was a choice to be like, yeah, we're just going to stay in, like, one time period.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.300
It's not related to anything else that's happened in this series so far.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.460
And, like, I get why people are upset with that, because you want more of it.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:52.600
But if you go into it and you're, like, you're okay with that, it's a good movie.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.160
I was looking for more of the Oedipal stuff.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.540
Yeah, well, I'll say that.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.120
I'll take two and three over one, regardless of that stuff.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.600
I just... One is just like, ah, yeah, that's the classic.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:05.760
It's like, you know...

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.780
That is crazy.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.600
One is so much better than two and three.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.040
One is, like... But it's the classic.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.000
Like, you've seen one so many times.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.760
When it comes out, I'm like, yeah, yeah, yeah, I've seen this.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.320
I want to see what happens next. That I understand.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.700
It's also, like, again, from my kid brain.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:26.080
Like, if I'm looking at them all, which is a better, more solid, like, just film, I'm sure it's one.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.220
But from my kid brain, I'm like, I want to see, like, time travel stuff.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:30.900
Interesting things from time travel.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.900
Yeah, seeing Jaws 3D is pretty freaking cool.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.800
And you don't really get so much of that. Yeah, right, right.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.100
And you see the future. You see an accurate representation of 2015.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.700
Yeah. It had the Cubs winning the World Series, but I think, like, a year later or something?

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.280
Yeah, that's why I can't take the movie seriously.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.680
Right, right. They came close.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.560
And, you know, there's obviously, there's a lot of funny things that came true as a result of Back to the Future Part 2.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:03.460
Like, those Nike self-tying shoes, like, eventually Nike did release those, but only, like, a hundred of them.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:07.620
And they made people cry, kind of like the Shishwan sauce from Rick and Morty.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.580
And Rick and Morty is basically just a ripoff of Back to the Future.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.180
That's true. So it's all full circle.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.540
But just to get back to the original question here, you know, we played Back to the Future Part 1,

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.020
and that was worthy of being its own game.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.120
Worthy in the sense that they managed to fill out.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.540
It had all the classic scenes.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.100
It was really a great tie-in.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.800
They filled out a full game.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.640
To answer your initial question, like, when you read that back of the box,

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.140
it immediately makes me think this is a Mario Duck Hunt situation.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:36.520
Like, there's two games in this?

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.660
They gave me both of these good games in this?

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.940
It's like, no. They've taken one move.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.900
Like, all right, I'll spoil it right here.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.100
I didn't beat this game.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:50.080
But just looking through all the footage, I'm like, wait, when does Back to the Future Part 2 end?

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.740
And Part 3 really begin here?

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.820
Like, it all kind of blends together.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.480
It's not like, it's not two separate games for the price of one.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.080
It's also not either of these movies.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:01.260
But we'll get into that.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:04.480
No, yeah. It's actually, I thought Joe was going to go in that direction.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.720
Joe, it is Mario.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:07.600
It is Mario.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.200
I don't know what is happening.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:20.560
But before we talk about the game, I would like to read you what I would consider the core gameplay of Back to the Future Part 2 and 3.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.220
Is this from the instruction manual?

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.300
It's a mix of the instruction manual and a little bit of paraphrasing here and there.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.480
So I'm just going to lay it all out to try and present to you the game as a whole.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.600
And then we'll talk about it.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.100
Does that sound fair? Yeah.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:50.060
Yes. It's a side-scrolling platformer and item collection game in which you travel through various times or years.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:57.120
1955, 1985, 2015, and eventually all the way back to 1885.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:06.880
And throughout this, you are platforming and stomping on enemies like you would Goombas or Koopas.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:23.400
And along the way, you find areas that become puzzle-solving mechanics where you have to collect a certain amount of a certain thing for the object to appear or do something within a time limit.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:36.600
And these little quick mini-like stages reward you with a prize that you must then bring that prize item to the correct location in order to fix the events.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:37.800
Time and location.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:40.520
What's that? Time and location.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:41.660
Yeah. Time and location.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.460
Right. To fix the events in the timelines.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:52.560
So there are multiple items to find across the various different stages.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.500
And then there's also different places to put them.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:05.740
All this to say, while that is the core game, this is a game where right away I just felt lost.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:06.880
Oh, absolutely.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.680
So. Yeah.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:09.620
Shall we?

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:21.440
Yes. Because I would like to know, you know, for me, I would say I gave up on the idea of this game being any good the second that I jumped.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:23.780
But. Yeah.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:33.160
I mean. But realistically, the most challenging part of this game is finding anything to do.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:40.280
So I very quickly realized that the game was a lot bigger than I was expecting.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:41.700
Or not even just bigger.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.260
Like, it was a lot more complex than I was expecting.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.240
Like, there's no, like, levels here.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:54.300
Really. There's, this is like one giant maze.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:55.360
Um.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:03.900
And basically, the, the, the plot reason why you're going to all these places and grabbing these things and bringing them back to the time and place is,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.060
Biff, Biff hit everything.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:10.180
And he's going to break the space time continuum if you don't fix it.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:13.120
Um. And I don't think that that's what happened in the movie.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.840
But fine. Um.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:28.500
But basically, you, they drop you off in 1985 and like some 1985 hellscape with Biff being like a, like a, a, a, a multi-millionaire entertainment mogul, whatever.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:35.760
And you have to just sort of navigate all of these very samey levels.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.100
Like, they are kind of levels in that they're like screens at least.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.320
Um. That some of them have landmarks.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:43.500
Most of them don't.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.740
You just kind of have to remember how the platforms are arranged.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:56.560
Um. And you, you have to go around and find these hidden rooms and find these hidden key items.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.360
But you are given no guidance.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:07.360
At all. And you are kind of expected to create a really complex map.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:12.600
And when I kind of, I figured out what, what was happening.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:22.740
Like, games like this, I usually can't even get like a, a very basic wraparound until I do some secondary research.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:28.040
And I found a video of this guy explaining like how he made a map.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:34.600
And it's this like 4D chess looking thing going back and forth between streets.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:43.780
And it's, it's really, it's, it's wildly complex for what I was expecting out of a movie tie-in game.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:48.120
Yeah. I think, I think you could even maybe say it's overly complex.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:57.360
Because it has, it's way more vast than it needs to be for what, for what is included in all of the vastness.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:58.560
If that makes any sense.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.520
Like, you have all these time periods.

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:01.680
And that's a cool idea.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.280
On the, you know, at base value.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:06.480
But you have all these time periods.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.740
But each time period is like a pretty long, I'd say full side-scroller level.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:16.180
And maybe then some length level.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:24.300
But then each of those has elevators that go like up and down to other just like almost identical looking full side-scroller levels.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:26.240
And, I mean, I don't even know.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.500
I feel like there's like 10 layers of these.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.460
There's 16. 16 layers of these.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:45.060
So here's the thing. Basically, those, in each of these screens, you'll have any number, like between one and four, I think, gateways that kind of bring you to a different street.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:48.680
Like, you're technically like all in the same neighborhood in this town.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.880
But they're all like the cross streets.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.100
And you're kind of going north and south on these streets.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:58.160
And there are 16 streets.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.820
And there are three versions of each.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:06.540
And the way that you get between them is different depending on the time period you're in.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.500
So they're kind of like mazes that have sections that are kind of cut off.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:12.940
So you have to... It's kind of a cool concept.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:17.160
And I remember Titanfall did something like this in the first-person shooter space.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.080
But we won't get into that.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.500
But it's the way that they've designed this.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.020
It's turned into backtracking the game.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:36.160
Yeah. Which is okay if it's like a really small space that has a lot of bespoke, highly detailed, fun elements.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:43.460
But not so fun if it is a very bland platforming level.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.400
Right. And it's like there are things to be found in it.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.220
But it's spread out.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.660
It's spread out over too much space.

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:59.840
So like all this space is like most of the time when you walk from one point to another, it was a waste of time.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:02.700
You know? Like unless you just know exactly where to go.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.580
Right. And that's the issue, right?

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.220
Is that the stages themselves, right?

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:19.100
So if you're in the alternate 1985 and you're walking around, you are doing this Super Mario Brothers style platforming a little bit.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:25.000
It doesn't feel anything like Super Mario Brothers other than the fact that you stomp on enemies and have a fireball power up.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.180
So I don't, I just, we could talk about why they decided to do that later.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:50.900
But like you're mostly just wandering around that hoping that you find the minigame portion of this that is like a smaller, a larger, smaller Marty in a big area where you have to collect items of like, you know, like 50 pizza slices or something.

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:56.380
And then as a result, you're rewarded with the piece of treasure.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.180
But that's all this game.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:03.120
Like that would have been the moment where like, oh, now you're doing something specific from the movie.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:10.200
Like now you're doing a hoverboard chase or now you're, um, uh, help me out.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:13.480
Another thing that happened, another action sequence in Back to the Future 2.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.520
Now you're jumping off a roof and landing at a DeLorean.

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:16.840
There you go. Yeah.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:27.540
It's like those kinds of things could have happened, but instead it just becomes this weird, like a different version of platforming and collecting that's very small and bite-sized.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:42.860
And then once you get that item now, it's like, okay, now go back out to the main world and just continue to roam around aimlessly, hoping that you've either find another one of those minigames or find a place to put this damn item that you just, that you just picked up.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:49.640
But it better be the right place because if you try to put it in the wrong one, you will lose it and have to go back and do the minigame again.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.640
Right. And so, and do that at minimum then like what?

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.120
There's like 30 of these items?

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:56.580
Yeah. So minimum 30 times.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:05.380
It's just, yeah, I'll say what, just watching the, you know, always after I finish one of these games, I watch like the YouTube, like long play of it.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.520
And usually it's like between 20 minutes to like an hour and a half.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:14.420
This is to beat this game, which I'm assuming someone who's very well versed is not necessarily a speed run, but someone who knows exactly where to go.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:16.480
Two hours and 58 minutes.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:18.920
It took three hours for like the expert to do it.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:19.840
Okay.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:34.640
But like the one thing I want to catch, I think like we get into this and we, we sort of come at this criticizing it from the point of view of three guys that have to play these games in a week.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:39.440
And it's like, why the fuck do they take this long?

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.320
Like, how can I be expected to do all this?

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:53.160
And like, I'm very, I'm, I'm, I'm very guilty of this, but I'm not even saying that like no game should be this elaborate.

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:54.880
No game should be this long.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.400
That's not what we're saying here.

00:13:56.440 --> 00:14:07.660
It, it seems to be this complex in this long with no context, like without any reason for that, without any reason for that being.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:18.160
And without any real thematic, uh, without any kind of thematic, uh, relation to the movie.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:37.140
Right. And just to agree real quick with you, Sean, like it's, it's not like, you know, if I see this length of time on this, this quote unquote speed run or expert run for metal gear or, or willow or something like, regardless of how I feel about those games, like that makes sense.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:41.780
That fits. And it could make sense for this, but it's like three hours of doing this.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:45.580
Like, it doesn't, it's just like, wow, that's, that's a lot of this.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.660
Right. That, that is the, that is the biggest problem.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.020
And, uh, you know, sorry to sound so negative about the game.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:15:00.440
We try to, uh, hide these a little bit, but like, it is, it is incredibly dull what is happening in this game at all times.

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:05.580
And I think that's because it called itself back to the future part two and three.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:10.740
And I barely feel like that character is Marty McFly in any way.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:11.880
Like, well, here's the thing.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:13.080
There's nothing happening.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:14.520
They got the DeLorean.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:15.780
Like the DeLorean.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.760
Sure, but you don't get to even fly the DeLorean.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:21.680
Like it just, it picks you up when you die and you can use it to travel to the other things.

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:25.640
But you get to interact with that cool, like, you know, uh, control panel.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:30.040
I also like how the DeLorean will sometimes just drop you off directly into like your death.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:35.000
Yeah. Like, you know, just like depending on where you die, it's like, we're going to drop you off exactly over this pit.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:46.320
But there is like one or two instances of the movie other than like, you know, forcing cut scenes that explain Biff taking the almanac and everything.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:52.380
Like there's only like one or two moments in this entire game that feel like they are tied in any way to back to the future.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.940
And that is the back to the future logo in the bottom left.

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:57.220
That's just always there.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:15:59.080
It's just always on screen.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:02.360
Oh, I forgot I was playing back to the future because it's nothing like it.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:04.880
Thank you for reminding me what I'm playing.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:15.700
And then the other instance is that, and this is just a, this is a, there was probably just one employee in this whole thing that cared about this game and said, guys, don't forget.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:18.220
We're making a back to the future movie, uh, video game.

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:22.560
We might as well like, you know, talk about like the consequences of time travel and everything.

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:31.960
And that is that when you do travel between 1955, 1985 and 2015, uh, no, no, that is, that is something else.

00:16:32.120 --> 00:16:41.420
But, um, no, this is that every time you do one of those time travels, uh, it creates a clone of Marty in the year in which you, uh, were.

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:47.040
So if you go back to that year, you might run into that clone, touch it and die.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:51.820
Just like how the, in the movie, they're not allowed to like see one another because it causes you to instantly faint.

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.220
Oh yeah. Like that's a thing.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:58.680
That, that's, you know, that is a good level of detail.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.020
It's not what I thought you were going to bring up.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:05.580
I, I'd forgotten about that, but like, I thought you were going to bring up the hoverboard, which is, it's not a hoverboard.

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:08.680
Such a half ass. It's just a moving platform.

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:11.580
It's a floating platform. It's a moving platform that disappears after a second.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:13.220
Anyways, it's just like, yeah, we put it in.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:14.600
It doesn't really help you in any way.

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:18.740
There's also all of the background elements, at least in 85.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.220
That's just like, you know, evil Biff.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.560
Uh, like lights everywhere.

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:26.600
